+  <section xml:id=3D"section-sumti-tcita-introduction">
+    <title>Introductory</title>
+    <para> <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>relationship</pri=
mary><secondary>objects of</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"gene=
ral-imported"><primary>relationship</primary><secondary>as basis of sentenc=
e</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>se=
ntence</primary><secondary>basic Lojban</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm=
 type=3D"general-imported"><primary>selbri</primary><secondary>definition</=
secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>sumti=
</primary><secondary>definition</secondary></indexterm> The basic type of L=
ojban sentence is the bridi: a claim by the speaker that certain objects ar=
e related in a certain way. The objects are expressed by Lojban grammatical=
 forms called=20
+    <valsi>sumti</valsi>; the relationship is expressed by the Lojban gram=
matical form called a=20
+    <valsi>selbri</valsi>.</para>
+    <para> <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>sumti</primary><s=
econdary>as objects in place structure slots</secondary></indexterm>  <inde=
xterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>place structure</primary><secondar=
y>empty slots in</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-import=
ed"><primary>place structure</primary><secondary>definition</secondary></in=
dexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>selbri</primary><se=
condary>place structure of</secondary></indexterm> The sumti are not random=
ly associated with the selbri, but according to a systematic pattern known =
as the=20
+    <quote>place structure</quote> of the selbri. This chapter describes t=
he various ways in which the place structure of Lojban bridi is expressed a=
nd by which it can be manipulated. The place structure of a selbri is a seq=
uence of empty slots into which the sumti associated with that selbri are p=
