-    <para><indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>FA selma'o</primar=
y><secondary>syntax of</secondary></indexterm> In sentences like=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-Ji94"/>, it is easy to get lost and=
 forget which sumti falls in which place, especially if the sumti are more =
complicated than simple names or descriptions. The place structure tags of =
selma'o FA may be used to help clarify place structures. The five cmavo=20
-    <valsi>fa</valsi>,=20
-    <valsi>fe</valsi>,=20
-    <valsi>fi</valsi>,=20
-    <valsi>fo</valsi>, and=20
-    <valsi>fu</valsi> may be inserted just before the sumti in the x1 to x=
5 places respectively:</para>
-    <example role=3D"interlinear-gloss-example" xml:id=3D"example-random-i=
d-yLop">
-      <title>
-        <anchor xml:id=3D"c9e3d1"/>
-      </title>
-      <interlinear-gloss>
-        <jbo>fa mi cu klama fe la bastn. fi la .atlantas. fo le dargu fu l=
e karce</jbo>
-        <gloss>x1=3D I go x2=3D Boston x3=3D Atlanta x4=3D the road x5=3D =
the car.</gloss>
-        <natlang>I go to Boston from Atlanta via the road using the car.</=
natlang>
-       =20
-       =20
-      </interlinear-gloss>
-    </example>
-    <para> <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>FA selma'o</prima=
ry><secondary>for accessing a selbri place explicitly by relative number</s=
econdary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>sumti<=
/primary><secondary>explicitly mapping into place structure with FA</second=
ary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>place struc=
ture</primary><secondary>explicitly mapping sumti to place with FA</seconda=
ry></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>place struct=
ure</primary><secondary>effect of FA on</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm=
 type=3D"general-imported"><primary>FA selma'o</primary><secondary>effect o=
n place structure</secondary></indexterm> In=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-yLop"/>, the tag=20
-    <valsi>fu</valsi> before=20
-    <jbophrase>le karce</jbophrase> clarifies that=20
-    <jbophrase>le karce</jbophrase> occupies the x5 place of=20
-    <valsi>klama</valsi>. The use of=20
-    <valsi>fu</valsi> tells us nothing about the purpose or meaning of the=
 x5 place; it simply says that=20
-    <jbophrase>le karce</jbophrase> occupies it.</para>
-    <para>In=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-yLop"/>, the tags are overkill; the=
y serve only to make=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-Ji94"/> even longer than it is. Her=
e is a better illustration of the use of FA tags for clarification:</para>
-    <example role=3D"interlinear-gloss-example" xml:id=3D"example-random-i=
d-3CPJ">
-      <title>
-        <anchor xml:id=3D"c9e3d2"/>
-      </title>
-      <interlinear-gloss>
-        <jbo>fa mi klama fe le zdani be mi be'o poi nurma vau fi la nu,IOR=
K.</jbo>
-        <gloss>x1=3D I go x2=3D (the house of me) which is-rural x3=3D New=
 York.</gloss>
-      </interlinear-gloss>
-    </example>
-    <para>In=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-3CPJ"/>, the place structure of=20
-    <valsi>klama</valsi> is as follows:</para>
-    <informaltable>
-      <colgroup/>
-          <tr>
-            <td>x1</td>
-            <td>agent</td>
-            <td><valsi>mi</valsi></td>
-          </tr>
-          <tr>
-            <td>x2</td>
-            <td>destination</td>
-            <td><jbophrase>le zdani be mi be'o poi nurma vau</jbophrase></=
td>
-          </tr>
-          <tr>
-            <td>x3</td>
-            <td>origin</td>
-            <td><jbophrase>la nu,IORK.</jbophrase></td>
-          </tr>
-          <tr>
-            <td>x4</td>
-            <td>route</td>
-            <td>(empty)</td>
-          </tr>
-          <tr>
-            <td>x5</td>
-            <td>means</td>
-            <td>(empty)</td>
-          </tr>
-    </informaltable>
-    <para> <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>FA selma'o</prima=
ry><secondary>as a reminder of place in place structure</secondary></indext=
erm> The=20
-    <valsi>fi</valsi> tag serves to remind the hearer that what follows is=
 in the x3 place of=20
-    <valsi>klama</valsi>; after listening to the complex sumti occupying t=
he x2 place, it's easy to get lost.</para>
-    <para> <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>sumti</primary><s=
econdary>re-ordering with FA</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"ge=
neral-imported"><primary>selbri-first bridi</primary><secondary>specifying =
first sumti place in with fa</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"ge=
neral-imported"><primary>place structure order</primary><secondary>effect o=
f FA on</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><prim=
ary>FA selma'o</primary><secondary>effect on place structure order</seconda=
ry></indexterm> Of course, once the sumti have been tagged, the order in wh=
ich they are specified no longer carries the burden of distinguishing the p=
laces. Therefore, it is perfectly all right to scramble them into any order=
 desired, and to move the selbri to anywhere in the bridi, even the beginni=
ng:</para>
-    <example role=3D"interlinear-gloss-example" xml:id=3D"example-random-i=
d-YmN2">
-      <title>
-        <anchor xml:id=3D"c9e3d3"/>
-      </title>
-      <interlinear-gloss>
-        <jbo>klama fa mi fi la .atlantas. fu le karce fe la bastn. fo le d=
argu</jbo>
-        <gloss>go x1=3D I x3=3D Atlanta x5=3D the car x2=3D Boston x4=3D t=
he road.</gloss>
-        <natlang>Go I from Atlanta using the car to Boston via the road.</=
natlang>
-      </interlinear-gloss>
-    </example>
-    <para><indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>selbri-first bridi=
</primary><secondary>effect on use of cu</secondary></indexterm>  <indexter=
m type=3D"general-imported"><primary>cu</primary><secondary>effect of selbr=
i-first bridi on</secondary></indexterm> Note that no=20
-    <valsi>cu</valsi> is permitted before the selbri in=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-YmN2"/>, because=20
-    <valsi>cu</valsi> separates the selbri from any preceding sumti, and=
=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-YmN2"/> has no such sumti.</para>
-    <example role=3D"interlinear-gloss-example" xml:id=3D"example-random-i=
d-fG8R">
-      <title>
-        <anchor xml:id=3D"c9e3d4"/>
-      </title>
-      <interlinear-gloss>
-        <jbo>fu le karce fo le dargu fi la .atlantas. fe la bastn. cu klam=
a fa mi</jbo>
-        <gloss>x5=3D the car x4=3D the road x3=3D Atlanta x2=3D Boston go =
x1=3DI</gloss>
-        <natlang>Using the car, via the road, from Atlanta to Boston go I.=
</natlang>
-      </interlinear-gloss>
-    </example>
-    <para>
-      <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-fG8R"/> exhibits the reverse of t=
he standard bridi form seen in=20
-     =20
-      <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-Ji94"/> and=20
-    <xref linkend=3D"example-random-id-yLop"/>, but still means exactly th=
e same thing. If the FA tags were left out, however, producing:</para>
-    <example role=3D"interlinear-gloss-example" xml:id=3D"example-random-i=
d-j7Nu">
-      <title>
-        <anchor xml:id=3D"c9e3d5"/>
-      </title>
-      <interlinear-gloss>
-        <jbo>le karce le dargu la .atlantas. la bastn. cu klama mi</jbo>
-        <gloss>The car to-the road from-Atlanta via-Boston goes using-me.<=
/gloss>
-        <natlang>The car goes to the road from Atlanta, with Boston as the=
 route, using me as a means of transport.</natlang>
-      </interlinear-gloss>
-    </example>
-    <para>the meaning would be wholly changed, and in fact nonsensical.</p=
ara>
-    <para> <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>zo'e</primary><se=
condary>compared with FA for omitting places</secondary></indexterm>  <inde=
xterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>FA selma'o</primary><secondary>com=
pared with zo'e for omitting places</secondary></indexterm>  <indexterm typ=
e=3D"general-imported"><primary>place structures</primary><secondary>omitti=
ng places with FA</secondary></indexterm> Tagging places with FA cmavo make=
s it easy not only to reorder the places but also to omit undesirable ones,=
 without any need for=20
-    <valsi>zo'e</valsi> or special rules about the x1 place:</para>
-    <example role=3D"interlinear-gloss-example" xml:id=3D"example-random-i=
d-brGX">
-      <title>
-        <anchor xml:id=3D"c9e3d6"/>
-      </title>
-      <interlinear-gloss>
-        <jbo>klama fi la .atlantas. fe la bastn. fu le karce</jbo>
-        <gloss>A-goer x3=3D Atlanta x2=3D Boston x5 =3D the car.</gloss>
-        <natlang>A goer from Atlanta to Boston using the car.</natlang>
-      </interlinear-gloss>
-    </example>
-    <para>Here the x1 and x4 places are empty, and so no sumti are tagged =
with=20
-    <valsi>fa</valsi> or=20
-    <valsi>fo</valsi>; in addition, the x2 and x3 places appear in reverse=
 order.</para>
-    <para> <indexterm type=3D"general-imported"><primary>FA selma'o</prima=
ry><secondary>effect on subsequent non-tagged places</secondary></indexterm=
> What if some sumti have FA tags and others do not? The rule is that after=
 a FA-tagged sumti, any sumti following it occupy the places numerically su=
cceeding it, subject to the proviso that an already-filled place is skipped=
:</para>
-    <example role=3D"interlinear-gloss-example" xml:id=3D"example-random-i=
